# Always Commit Work

Create a git commit when the user has asked for one. Do not commit only because a change is finished.

## When to Commit

- When the user has asked for a commit after a feature, fix, or refactor
- When the user has asked for a commit after files were created or deleted
- When the user has asked for a commit after configuration or dependencies changed

## Commit Message Format

Use the imperative mood and be specific:

```
# ✅ GOOD
git commit -m "Add user authentication flow"
git commit -m "Fix null pointer in payment handler"
git commit -m "Remove deprecated API endpoints"

# ❌ BAD
git commit -m "changes"
git commit -m "fix"
git commit -m "wip"
```

## Rules

- Stage all relevant files before committing (`git add`)
- Never use `--no-verify` to skip hooks unless explicitly asked
- Never force-push to `main`/`master`
- One logical change per commit — don't batch unrelated changes
